  Ugh. So the container state is genuinely undecidable from local evidence. Both scenarios are coherent. Therefore the correct review finding is: r2 inherited A1's unverified production-state premise ("runner 无此调用") whose only adjudicated evidence was the audit default (which proves nothing about the runner), and local git evidence (f691abb ancestor of the r181 guard commit) makes the premise doubtful. Since the consequence of the premise being wrong is an unplanned G2 activation (real JHEMR writes) with waived acceptance, R1 should add a one-line container probe: `grep -c sync_jhemr_login_sign_daily /app/scripts/run_identity_modified_nightly.py` → if ≥1, STOP 呈报（or explicitly cover login_sign acceptance + user authorization). Given 铁律 "与方案不符的事实=STOP", actually if the runner has the call and login_sign executes, would the executor even notice? R4.3 says 不要求 login_sign — the executor wouldn't check. The run would perform G2 writes silently. That's why I'd rate this a substantive new finding — arguably blocking-level for a plan whose 非目标 explicitly excludes G2 activation, because there's a realistic, evidence-backed scenario where executing the plan as written violates its own 非目标 without detection.

  Is it "blocking"? The user's question asks: ②有无新增阻断级问题. I'd classify it as 阻断级（新）or at least 必须修复： add the R1 probe. Given uncertainty, I'll present it as "新增高严重度问题（建议视为阻断，因修复成本极低：R1 加一条 grep）".

